1	// Fault-injection shims for the regression-test build of envelopefile.
2	//
3	// This file is linked together with src/main.cpp into the separate
4	// `envelopefile_testable` binary using the linker's --wrap option, so every
5	// open/write/fsync/close/unlink call made by the product code is routed
6	// through the __wrap_* functions below. The production binary is completely
7	// unaffected.
8	//
9	// Injection is controlled entirely through environment variables so the test
10	// driver can select a scenario per process run:
11	//
12	//   EF_TEST_TARGET          Only inject faults for this exact path. When
13	//                           unset, any file created with O_CREAT|O_EXCL is
14	//                           considered the key file under test.
15	//   EF_TEST_FAIL_WRITE=1    write() on the key file fails with EIO.
16	//   EF_TEST_PARTIAL_WRITE=N write() on the key file returns at most N bytes
17	//                           per call (the caller must loop to complete).
18	//   EF_TEST_FAIL_FSYNC=1    fsync() on the key file fails with EIO.
19	//   EF_TEST_FAIL_CLOSE=1    close() on the key file reports EIO (the
20	//                           descriptor is still really closed, matching the
21	//                           POSIX guarantee that close() releases the fd).
22	//   EF_TEST_FAIL_UNLINK=1   unlink() of the key file fails with EIO after a
23	//                           save failure, simulating cleanup that cannot
24	//                           remove the incomplete file the run just created.
25	//                           The key file is really left on disk. Only the
26	//                           path created by this run is affected: unlinks of
27	//                           any other path pass through untouched.
28	//   EF_TEST_FAIL_OPEN=1     the key file's open(O_CREAT|O_EXCL) fails BEFORE
29	//                           any file is created — the real syscall is never
30	//                           reached — simulating a save location that refuses
31	//                           to accept the new file at creation time. The errno
32	//                           defaults to EACCES (a parent directory that denies
33	//                           creation) and can be overridden with the decimal
34	//                           value in EF_TEST_OPEN_ERRNO. Because the refusal
35	//                           is injected, the scenario is real even when the
36	//                           test process runs with effective permission in the
37	//                           directory (e.g. as root), and nothing is left on
38	//                           disk, so the product's pre-creation ("not created")
39	//                           path is exercised rather than its post-creation
40	//                           cleanup. Only the O_CREAT|O_EXCL key-file open is
41	//                           matched, so log files and every other open pass
42	//                           through untouched.
43	//   EF_TEST_FAIL_FCHMOD=1   fchmod() on the key file fails with EPERM, so the
44	//                           0600 permission guarantee cannot be established.
45	//   EF_TEST_FAIL_FSTAT=1    fstat() on the key file fails with EIO even though
46	//                           fchmod() reported success: the post-set permission
47	//                           verification cannot read the file's metadata.
48	//   EF_TEST_FSTAT_MODE=mmm  fstat() succeeds but the permission bits reported
49	//                           back are replaced with the octal mode mmm (only the
50	//                           low 12 bits are taken; file type bits are kept),
51	//                           simulating a filesystem where fchmod() reports
52	//                           success but the effective mode differs, e.g. 0644
53	//                           (group/other can still read) or 0400 (owner write
54	//                           was stripped).
55	//
56	// For deterministic coverage of the write loop itself, a per-call script can
57	// be supplied:
58	//
59	//   EF_TEST_WRITE_SCRIPT=t1,t2,...
60	//                           Each token describes the outcome of one write()
61	//                           call on the key file, consumed in order; once the
62	//                           list is exhausted every further call succeeds
63	//                           normally. Tokens:
64	//                             ok    - write the whole requested buffer
65	//                             N     - short write: at most N (decimal) bytes
66	//                             intr  - fail with EINTR, no bytes written
67	//                             eio   - fail irrecoverably with EIO
68	//                             zero  - return 0 with bytes still pending
69	//
70	//   EF_TEST_WRITE_LOG=path  Record what the write loop actually did, so the
71	//                           test driver can prove byte continuity across
72	//                           retries. On the first key-file write the original
73	//                           32-byte key is snapshotted; every subsequent call
74	//                           is checked to advance from the current offset with
75	//                           a buffer that is a slice of that same snapshot
76	//                           (i.e. the key is neither regenerated, duplicated,
77	//                           nor dropped). Every call is also checked to pass a
78	//                           pointer INTO the exact buffer RAND_bytes filled
79	//                           (at the current offset), so the bytes that reach
80	//                           the file are provably the ones the later
81	//                           OPENSSL_cleanse wipes — not a copy living
82	//                           elsewhere. The file receives one line:
83	//                             VERIFY <64 lowercase hex digits>
84	//                           followed by one line per write() call:
85	//                             CALL <offset> <requested> <returned> [<errno>]
86	//                           (the errno field is only present when returned is
87	//                           -1). A continuity violation is recorded as a
88	//                             VERIFY-FAILED <reason>
89	//                           line and aborts the process, since it means the
90	//                           product code violated its own write contract.
91	//
92	// The secure random source can be made to fail deterministically:
93	//
94	//   EF_TEST_FAIL_RAND=1       RAND_bytes reports failure (returns 0).
95	//   EF_TEST_RAND_PARTIAL=N    before failing, N marker bytes (0xA5) are
96	//                             written into the caller's key buffer,
97	//                             simulating a CSPRNG that dies mid-fill. The
98	//                             marker lets the test driver prove the partial
99	//                             data is neither saved nor leaked.
100	//   EF_TEST_RAND_ERROR_DETAIL=1
101	//                             leave a descriptive OpenSSL error on the
102	//                             thread's error queue so the failure carries
103	//                             detail text; when unset the queue is cleared
104	//                             instead, exercising the no-detail message.
105	//
106	// Key-buffer lifecycle events are recorded when requested:
107	//
108	//   EF_TEST_KEY_LOG=path      one line per event:
109	//                               RAND <requested> partial=<N> result=<ok|fail>
110	//                               CLEANSED-KEY <len>
111	//                               CLEANSE-STATE writes=<N> keyfd=<open|closed>
112	//                             The CLEANSED-KEY line is emitted when the
113	//                             buffer previously handed to RAND_bytes is
114	//                             wiped via OPENSSL_cleanse, proving the key
115	//                             (including any partial bytes) does not survive
116	//                             the operation in memory. It is followed by a
117	//                             CLEANSE-STATE line capturing how many key bytes
118	//                             had been written and whether the key-file
119	//                             descriptor was already closed at that moment,
120	//                             so the test driver can prove the wipe happened
121	//                             only after the save was complete (never before,
122	//                             which would save wiped data).
123	//
124	// Only the file descriptor opened for the key file is instrumented; all
125	// other I/O (stdout, stderr, OpenSSL internals, the log file itself) passes
126	// through untouched.
